.Dd August 14, 2026
.Dt VIRTIO_IOMMU 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm virtio_iommu
.Nd VirtIO IOMMU driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel,
place the following lines in your
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "device virtio_iommu"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the driver as a
module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
virtio_iommu_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
device driver provides support for VirtIO IOMMU (virtio-iommu) devices, the
paravirtual input/output memory management unit defined by the VirtIO
specification.
.Pp
The driver implements the guest side of the virtio-iommu protocol.
It owns a request virtqueue, over which it issues the
ATTACH, DETACH, MAP, UNMAP and PROBE commands with full status handling, and
an event virtqueue, on which it receives asynchronous fault reports from the
device.
It maintains a domain, endpoint and mapping model reflecting the state it has
programmed into the device.
Both split and packed virtqueues are supported transparently by the shared
VirtIO transport layer.
.Ss Translation boundary
The complete, exercised deliverable of this driver is the virtio-iommu
.Em protocol .
The driver does
.Em not
yet reprogram other devices'
.Xr busdma 9
so that their DMA is transparently translated through this IOMMU.
In
.Fx
that transparency is provided by the busdma/IOMMU framework driven from
firmware topology tables; for virtio-iommu specifically it requires an ACPI
VIOT table parser that binds endpoint identifiers to this unit and a
busdma back end whose map and unmap operations call into this driver's request
queue.
Those components live in the shared framework and are not provided here, so
real host translation is not yet wired up.
The driver exposes a lookup seam that resolves an endpoint identifier to its
protocol engine, so a future in-tree VIOT and busdma back end can drive real
translation without changing the protocol core.
.Pp
Because a non-translating IOMMU provides a guest no isolation, this guest
driver is
.Em disabled by default
and declines the device unless the operator opts in with
.Va hw.virtio_iommu.enable Ns = Ns 1 .
It is intended for exercising the virtio-iommu protocol and the in-tree
.Xr bhyve 8
host model, not as a guest DMA-isolation capability.
The production deliverable is the host-side virtio-iommu model, which presents
a functional IOMMU
.Pq with an ACPI VIOT table binding its endpoints
to guests whose operating system can consume one, such as Linux;
.Fx
guests are limited by the absence of the VIOT and
.Xr busdma 9
back end described above.
.Sh LOADER TUNABLES
The following
.Xr loader 8
tunable, read at boot, controls the driver:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.virtio_iommu.enable
When set to a non-zero value, the driver attaches to virtio-iommu devices.
The default value is 0: the driver declines the device and does not attach,
because it implements the protocol but does not provide transparent
.Xr busdma 9
translation for other devices.
Set it to 1 to exercise the protocol against a virtio-iommu device.
.El
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The following read-only variables are available as
.Xr sysctl 8
variables, per attached device, and count protocol activity.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va dev.vtiommu. Ns Ar X Ns Va .attach_reqs
The number of ATTACH requests issued.
.It Va dev.vtiommu. Ns Ar X Ns Va .detach_reqs
The number of DETACH requests issued.
.It Va dev.vtiommu. Ns Ar X Ns Va .map_reqs
The number of MAP requests issued.
.It Va dev.vtiommu. Ns Ar X Ns Va .unmap_reqs
The number of UNMAP requests issued.
.It Va dev.vtiommu. Ns Ar X Ns Va .probe_reqs
The number of PROBE requests issued.
.It Va dev.vtiommu. Ns Ar X Ns Va .faults
The number of fault events received on the event virtqueue.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr virtio 4 ,
.Xr busdma 9
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Fx 15.0 .
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm
driver was written by
.An Kory Heard .
.Sh BUGS
The driver implements the virtio-iommu protocol but does not yet provide
transparent
.Xr busdma 9
translation for other devices; see the
.Sx DESCRIPTION .
